(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2609101800-one-declaration-one-reader-one-reconciler")
  (:adr/title "The fleet's topology: one declaration, one reader, one reconciler")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-10")
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609101600-which-models-run-stably-on-16-gib"
                "adr-2609101100-a-node-dedicated-to-one-model"
                "adr-2609100100-one-model-per-node"))
  (:adr/body
  "Owner instruction 2026-09-10: tidy the topology so the cluster runs
efficiently and stably.

## The field was already there, and nobody read it

`fleet.edn` carries `:node/serves` on every node. Eleven of the twelve said
`:unstated`, one said a bare model id, and a grep across murakumo and the API
for a reader returned nothing. The fleet had a slot for its own topology,
empty, with no consumer -- and every symptom measured that day follows from
it:

- five minis each ran four model planes, and nothing said that was wrong
- the media plane on those minis had rendered NOTHING, ever: dan's ComfyUI
  showed 0 history entries with its checkpoint unloaded, benjamin's and
  simeon's did not answer, and simeon had no checkpoint at all
- the fleet console showed every node as `qwen35-iq2:latest`, a model none of
  them was serving, while its own API underneath had the per-node truth
- a `llama-server` appeared on dan holding 2.6 GB on :8094 with parent PID 1
  and no launchd job; only a process listing found it

None of those is a machine failing. They are a fleet with no answer to *what
is this node for*.

## The shape

    murakumo edge topology [<node>|all] [--apply]

`murakumo.fleet.topology` holds a closed vocabulary and the verdict;
`murakumo.fleet.one-model` measures; `edge-install` runs it. The declaration:

    :unstated                                     nobody has decided
    :none                                         deliberately idle
    {:plane :text  :model \"murakumo-edge\"}
    {:plane :media :model \"waiREALMIX_v11.safetensors\"}
    {:plane :ring-member}
    {:plane :ad-hoc}

**`:unstated` is not a default and is never folded into a pass.** A node
nobody has decided about is not in the right state and not in the wrong state,
and a reconciler that picked either would churn it or bless it. The fleet
summary prints the count and says it in words, because `drift=0` on a fleet of
undecided nodes reads as health. `:unmeasured` is likewise never conformant.

`--apply` only ever EVICTS. Starting a plane needs a model, a checkpoint and
an admission check, which `dedicate-node!` owns; a missing plane is reported
and left rather than half-started by a function whose job is tidying.

## Two defects this replaces, both found by running it on the fleet

**The plane-to-label table was a fixed guess.** benjamin serves the ad-hoc
plane from `ai.gftd.ollama.benjamin` where dan and simeon use
`com.murakumo.ollama`. An eviction driven by the table left ollama running on
benjamin and reported a clean sweep; only the `MURAKUMO_STILL_LOADED` guard
caught it. Labels are now DISCOVERED by reading every plist's
ProgramArguments and classifying on the executable path -- the same check that
was the only one that worked in the 2026-09-09 gftd cutover, for the same
reason: a label says what somebody called a job, the argv says what it runs.

**A missing label had two meanings and one response.** Not running means
nothing to evict; running with no job means refuse. `evict-labels` now takes
the measured planes so it can tell them apart. The second case is dan's
orphan 27B, and a tool that reported `nothing to evict` would have called that
node tidy. Without the measurement the refusal fired on every safe eviction
instead -- which is how the first version behaved on judah.

A third, smaller: `evict-script` counted still-loaded jobs the instant after
`bootout`, which returns before launchd finishes unloading. It reported 1 and
2 jobs still loaded on simeon and dan; both were 0 seconds later with every
plist parked. It now settles first. A guard that cries wolf is a guard an
operator learns to skip.

## The topology as declared and measured, 2026-09-10

    judah      text   murakumo-edge              conformant (reconciled)
    benjamin   media  waiREALMIX_v11             conformant
    simeon     media  waiREALMIX_v11             conformant
    dan        media  waiREALMIX_v11             drift -- REFUSED, see below
    gad        media  waiREALMIX_v11             drift -- REFUSED, no launchd
    issachar   text   murakumo-27b               unmeasured (dark)
    xavier     text   qwen3-vl-30b-a3b           unmeasured (dark)
    naphtali zebulun levi joseph asher           unstated (all dark)

    fleet: conformant=3 drift=2 unstated=5 unmeasured=2

dan is refused because the text plane on it has no launchd job: it is another
session's 27B, started 14:02 that day from a path and port this work never
used, and CLAUDE.md's rule on other agents' work products applies. gad is
refused because the discovery script reads macOS plists and gad is Linux --
honest, and it means gad can never be reconciled by this tool.

## What the image side now is

benjamin, simeon and dan carry WAI (`waiREALMIX_v11`, 6.94 GB) and nothing
else; benjamin and simeon are WAI-only on disk. Measured warm at 768x768,
25 steps: 72 s/image on all three. At 1024x1024 the same model takes 152 s and
grows swap by 1,675 MB with 5.94 GB compressed, which `model-fit` calls
`:swapping`. **768 is the operating point, and it is a property of the
resolution, not of the checkpoint.**

Converting the two port-exhausted nodes was the right way round for a reason
that had to be measured to be believed: image work is local, but a localhost
TCP connection still takes an ephemeral port, and with the range full neither
node could reach its own ComfyUI (0 of 5 curls). Evicting ollama freed 11434
and let the floor drop to 9000; both went to 5 of 5.

## Still open

- **hokusai is not published.** `/v1/images/generations` works and routes
  through `GATEWAY_URL` to gad's bridge; `awai-network/hokusai` returns 503 by
  design in `dead_hosted_model.js`; and the fleet-aware `infer-gateway`
  (`pick-any-node!`, which would select a WAI mini by checkpoint) is running on
  NO node -- job absent and :8790 unbound everywhere. Publishing hokusai on the
  minis needs that gateway deployed and fronted; publishing it on gad needs one
  config change. Owner decision.
- **The fleet console's HTML lies.** `/api/state` has correct per-node
  residents; the page renders `qwen35-iq2:latest / advertised, not loaded` on
  every row. The API is right and the page is wrong, uniformly, which is the
  worst way to be wrong.
- issachar and joseph are up, on the LAN, with port 22 open and no credential
  that reaches them.
- The heartbeat backoff still crosses its own deadline (30 s cadence, first
  retry at 60-75 s, freshness line at 60 s), and the control plane still
  carries two disagreeing thresholds (60 s and 120 s).")))
